---
// Local AI for Type Design with the Runebender Font Editor. Workshop
// slides for ATypI 2026 Sharjah. Part 1 is a short demo. Part 2 is
// hands-on setup on the attendees' own machines.
// One idea per slide, in large type. Details, numbers, and commands are
// in the speaker notes (press P for the presenter window).
// Companion to runebender.org/docs/local-ai-setup.html and the demo repo.
import Fig from '@/components/slides/Fig.astro'
import Slide from '@/components/slides/Slide.astro'
import SlidesLayout from '@/layouts/SlidesLayout.astro'

import endRunebender from '@/assets/slides/localai/end-runebender.jpg'
import grid from '@/assets/slides/localai/runebender-xilem-grid.png'
import nodes from '@/assets/slides/localai/runebender-xilem-nodes.png'
import nufoEditing from '@/assets/slides/localai/runebender-xilem-nufo-editing.png'
import outline from '@/assets/slides/localai/runebender-xilem-outline.png'
import titleDgxSpark from '@/assets/slides/localai/title-dgx-spark.jpg'
---

<SlidesLayout
  title="Local AI for Type Design with the Runebender Font Editor (slides)"
  description="Workshop slides for Local AI for Type Design with the Runebender Font Editor, ATypI 2026 Sharjah."
  running="Local AI for Type Design"
  author="Eli Heuer"
>
  <!-- ── Opening ── -->

  <Slide label="Title">
    <img
      class="bleed"
      src={titleDgxSpark.src}
      alt="Two NVIDIA DGX Spark units stacked on a desk, linked by two QSFP cables, with a keyboard behind them."
    />
    <div class="c-1-9 over-bleed">
      <h1 class="t-display descends">Local AI for<br />Type Design</h1>
      <p class="t-head">with the Runebender Font Editor</p>
    </div>
    <div class="c-1-6 over-bleed at-bottom">
      <p class="t-body">Eli Heuer · ATypI 2026 Sharjah</p>
    </div>
    <Fragment slot="notes">
      <p>Runebender is an open-source, cross-platform font editor written in Rust. It runs, trains, and fine-tunes models on your own machine.</p>
      <p>Setup instructions are at runebender.org/docs/local-ai-setup.html. The exercises are in the runebender-local-ai-demo repository.</p>
    </Fragment>
  </Slide>

  <Slide section="Overview" label="Format">
    <div class="c-1-10 stack-lg">
      <h2 class="t-display">Format</h2>
      <p class="t-head">Part 1: a short demo of Runebender with local models.</p>
      <p class="t-head">Part 2: hands-on setup on your own Mac or Linux PC.</p>
    </div>
    <Fragment slot="notes">
      <p>The demo covers training a model from scratch, fine-tuning, open-weight chat models, and the node editor.</p>
      <p>Setup routes: a DMG on a Mac, a few terminal commands on Mac or Linux, or a coding agent.</p>
    </Fragment>
  </Slide>

  <!-- ── Core concepts ── -->

  <Slide section="Concepts" label="Why local">
    <div class="c-1-6 stack">
      <h2 class="t-display">Why local</h2>
      <p class="t-head">
        Small models run on a laptop. You control the training data, the
        models, and what reaches the font.
      </p>
    </div>
    <Fig
      class="c-7-12 fig-right"
      src={nufoEditing}
      alt="Runebender editing Arabic glyphs with a rendered preview."
    />
    <Fragment slot="notes">
      <p>A PC with a good graphics card can train larger models. The editor downloads nothing and sends nothing.</p>
      <p>The font models are one to a few dozen megabytes. Training one from scratch takes minutes on a laptop GPU.</p>
      <p>Most font generation research trains on large collections of scraped fonts, which mix many drawing conventions. These models train on the designer’s own drawings, made on one grid.</p>
    </Fragment>
  </Slide>

  <Slide section="Concepts" label="Models are folders">
    <div class="c-1-10 stack-lg">
      <h2 class="t-display">Models are folders</h2>
      <p class="t-head">
        A model is a folder of files on your disk. Runebender reads it from
        <code>~/Runebender/models</code> or from beside the font.
      </p>
    </div>
    <Fragment slot="notes">
      <p>A Runebender model folder holds a config, the weights, a vocabulary, and sometimes a manifest. A chat model folder holds a GGUF file.</p>
      <p>Folders are sorted by kind: shapes, bolden, chat. A font’s own models are in MyFont.models/ beside it, so they move with the font.</p>
      <p>The editor reads only these files. The config is plain JSON. RUNEBENDER_MODELS sets a different root for one run.</p>
    </Fragment>
  </Slide>

  <Slide section="Concepts" label="The training loop">
    <div class="c-1-10 stack-lg">
      <h2 class="t-display">The training loop</h2>
      <p class="t-head">
        You draw a core set of glyphs. The model drafts the rest. You correct
        the drafts, and they become new training data.
      </p>
    </div>
    <Fragment slot="notes">
      <ul>
        <li>Draw and grade a reference set in one master, marked green.</li>
        <li>Train on the green set only.</li>
        <li>Draft 10 to 20 glyphs on the 8-unit grid, as a proposal.</li>
        <li>Correct, install, and mark orange.</li>
        <li>Grade corrected drafts green. They join the training set.</li>
        <li>Build the font and proof it in text.</li>
        <li>Name each recurring error and add a check for it before the next run.</li>
      </ul>
    </Fragment>
  </Slide>

  <Slide section="Concepts" label="Drafts are proposals">
    <div class="c-1-10 stack-lg">
      <h2 class="t-display">Drafts are proposals</h2>
      <p class="t-head">
        A model never edits the font directly. You install or discard each
        draft, and only you save.
      </p>
    </div>
    <Fragment slot="notes">
      <p>Only a person marks a glyph green. Machine drafts stay orange until they are graded.</p>
      <p>Drafts land on the 8-unit grid. The designer makes the 2-unit optical corrections.</p>
      <p>Each engine is scored against drawn pairs. The baseline is a plain mean shift.</p>
      <p>The same rules apply to local models, to agents over MCP, and to the Chat panel.</p>
    </Fragment>
  </Slide>

  <!-- ── Part 1: demo ── -->

  <Slide section="Demo" label="Demo">
    <img
      class="bleed"
      src={endRunebender.src}
      alt="Runebender with a labeled bismillah and its preview."
    />
    <div class="c-1-9 over-bleed">
      <p class="t-display">Demo</p>
    </div>
    <Fragment slot="notes">
      <p>Switch to Runebender. Order: open Shapes.ufo, train a shapes model from scratch, add a shape to fine-tune it, bolden octagon and chevron, ask the chat model about the font by voice, run the bolden graph in the Nodes tab.</p>
      <p>The slides that follow are reference for the same steps. Slides marked Planned describe features that are not finished.</p>
    </Fragment>
  </Slide>

  <Slide section="Demo" label="Training from scratch">
    <div class="c-1-6 stack">
      <h2 class="t-display">Training from scratch</h2>
      <p class="t-head">
        Train a model on the shapes in your font. Then drag a box, choose a
        shape, and the model draws it.
      </p>
    </div>
    <Fig
      class="c-7-12 fig-right"
      src={outline}
      alt="Runebender editing an outline, with the right panel open."
    />
    <Fragment slot="notes">
      <p>Open a glyph, hold the shapes button, and choose Local AI Shapes. Without a model, the panel offers Train From the Sample Shapes.</p>
      <p>After training, Enter keeps the preview, Tab draws another, Esc removes it.</p>
      <p>The model can draw only the shapes in the training font. Each glyph name is the word for its shape.</p>
      <p>A quick training run takes about twelve minutes on a laptop GPU. A thorough run takes about thirty. get-base-model.sh in the demo repository downloads a trained base model with a checksum.</p>
    </Fragment>
  </Slide>

  <Slide section="Demo" label="Fine-tuning">
    <div class="c-1-10 stack-lg">
      <h2 class="t-display">Fine-tuning</h2>
      <p class="t-head">
        Continue training an existing model with new drawings. It needs less
        data and less time than training from scratch.
      </p>
    </div>
    <Fragment slot="notes">
      <p>In the editor: draw a new shape and click Add This Font’s Shapes. Training continues from the current model for about two minutes.</p>
      <p>In font-ml, an adapter is a small set of weights trained over a frozen base model, in the shape of a LoRA:</p>
      <p>font-ml run train --source Regular.ufo --target Bold.ufo --init my-bolden --adapter-out my-adapter --rank 8</p>
      <p>font-ml run bolden --model my-bolden --source Regular.ufo --glyph H --adapter my-adapter:1.0</p>
      <p>The Nodes tab includes a train-adapter graph that runs the same training.</p>
    </Fragment>
  </Slide>

  <Slide section="Demo" label="Fine-tuning in the editor" planned>
    <div class="c-1-10 stack-lg">
      <h2 class="t-display">Fine-tuning in the editor</h2>
      <p class="t-head">
        A Finetune button trains a small adapter on the glyphs you graded
        green. Bolden drafts use it if it scores better.
      </p>
    </div>
    <Fragment slot="notes">
      <p>The panel shows whether each script has enough drawn pairs: 20 green pairs, with 5 held out for scoring.</p>
      <p>The adapter is saved in MyFont.models/ beside the font.</p>
      <p>Today the same training runs from font-ml and from the train-adapter graph.</p>
    </Fragment>
  </Slide>

  <Slide section="Demo" label="Bolden">
    <div class="c-1-6 stack">
      <h2 class="t-display">Bolden</h2>
      <p class="t-head">
        The model moves the points of a Regular glyph to draft its Bold. The
        masters still interpolate.
      </p>
    </div>
    <Fig
      class="c-7-12 fig-right"
      src={grid}
      alt="Runebender’s glyph overview grid for a Latin font."
    />
    <Fragment slot="notes">
      <p>The Weight debt block lists Bold glyphs that are still identical to Regular. It drafts them in batches of 20 and marks them orange. About half a second per glyph on Metal.</p>
      <p>Measured 2026-10-09 on Virtua Grotesk: Latin 56 drawn pairs, model and offset about even. Arabic 130 pairs, offset better. Hebrew 5 pairs, offset better. Both engines are only slightly better than the mean shift.</p>
      <p>An engine needs 20 green pairs on a script, with 5 held out, before its drafts are used.</p>
    </Fragment>
  </Slide>

  <Slide section="Demo" label="Drafting from a sketch" planned>
    <div class="c-1-10 stack-lg">
      <h2 class="t-display">Drafting from a sketch</h2>
      <p class="t-head">
        Sketch a glyph with the brush. A drawing model redraws it in the
        style of your font, as a proposal.
      </p>
    </div>
    <Fragment slot="notes">
      <p>Steps: brush on the glyph canvas, trace with img2bez, redraw with the drawing model through font-ml, review in the proposal layer.</p>
      <p>The chat model does not draw. The drawing model trains on the glyphs graded green.</p>
      <p>The Brush panel already traces temporary ink and can draft with the Virtua sketch model. The first trial, on 2026-09-28, lost the form of the medial kaf. The drawing model is the open work.</p>
    </Fragment>
  </Slide>

  <Slide section="Demo" label="Open-weight chat models">
    <div class="c-1-10 stack-lg">
      <h2 class="t-display">Open-weight chat models</h2>
      <p class="t-head">
        Open-weight models run on your own machine. In the Chat panel, they
        answer questions about the open font.
      </p>
    </div>
    <Fragment slot="notes">
      <p>The Chat panel uses GGUF files, such as Qwen3 from Hugging Face, run by llama.cpp.</p>
      <p>Small models cannot calculate a stem width from a point list. They do call the correct tools in the correct order. The tools must return the measurement directly.</p>
      <p>Test on Virtua Grotesk, M4 Pro, stem of H. Candle runtime, Qwen3 4B: 543 s, wrong. llama-server, Qwen3 4B: 11 s, wrong. llama-server, Qwen3 8B: 24 s, wrong. llama-server with analyze_glyph, Qwen3 4B: 5 s, correct (100 units).</p>
      <p>A 0.6B model gives wrong answers. The Chat panel was built and tested with 4B.</p>
    </Fragment>
  </Slide>

  <Slide section="Demo" label="Model catalog" planned>
    <div class="c-1-10 stack-lg">
      <h2 class="t-display">Model catalog</h2>
      <p class="t-head">
        Choose a chat model in the Chat panel and download it with one click.
      </p>
    </div>
    <Fragment slot="notes">
      <p>Each entry shows the size, license, and source. The download comes from Hugging Face and is checked against its sha256 checksum. Nothing downloads without the click.</p>
      <p>Catalog decided 2026-10-18, all Apache 2.0: Qwen3.5 4B (about 2.7 GB, 8 GB machines, default), Qwen3.5 9B (about 5.5 GB, 16 GB), Qwen3.6 35B A3B (about 20 GB, 32 GB).</p>
      <p>A GGUF file placed in a folder under chat/ also works. Thinking is off by default, with a switch in the composer.</p>
    </Fragment>
  </Slide>

  <Slide section="Demo" label="Voice input">
    <div class="c-1-10 stack-lg">
      <h2 class="t-display">Voice input</h2>
      <p class="t-head">
        Speak a request. A local speech model writes it in the chat field for
        you to check.
      </p>
    </div>
    <Fragment slot="notes">
      <p>The Talk button uses whisper-server from whisper.cpp (brew install whisper.cpp) and a Whisper model saved as ~/Runebender/models/whisper-small/model.bin.</p>
      <p>The button is hidden until both are found. Audio stays on the machine.</p>
    </Fragment>
  </Slide>

  <Slide section="Demo" label="The node editor">
    <div class="c-1-6 stack">
      <h2 class="t-display">The node editor</h2>
      <p class="t-head">
        Connect nodes into a workflow, as in ComfyUI. A workflow is a file you
        can share and run again.
      </p>
    </div>
    <Fig
      class="c-7-12 fig-right"
      src={nodes}
      alt="Runebender’s Nodes tab with a workflow graph."
    />
    <Fragment slot="notes">
      <p>Nodes for the font, a model, a master, and tasks such as Bolden, Compare, and Install.</p>
      <p>A workflow runs in the Nodes tab or from the command line. A run does not save or change the open font. Results are candidates until they are installed.</p>
      <p>External tasks declare their inputs, so unchanged runs are cached.</p>
    </Fragment>
  </Slide>

  <!-- ── Part 2: hands-on ── -->

  <Slide section="Hands-on" label="Hands-on setup">
    <div class="c-1-10 stack-lg">
      <h2 class="t-display">Hands-on setup</h2>
      <p class="t-head">
        Install Runebender and run a first model on your own machine.
      </p>
    </div>
  </Slide>

  <!-- ── Hardware ── -->

  <Slide section="Hardware" label="Memory size">
    <div class="c-1-10 stack-lg">
      <h2 class="t-display">Memory size</h2>
      <p class="t-head">
        The model must fit in memory. A 4B model needs about 3 GB. A 35B model
        needs about 22 GB.
      </p>
    </div>
    <Fragment slot="notes">
      <p>For people new to local models: the processor matters less than the size and speed of the memory the model can use.</p>
      <p>At 4 bits, a model takes about 0.6 GB per billion parameters, plus space for the context.</p>
      <p>My chat models, 4-bit GGUF: qwen3.5-4b 2.9 GB, gpt-oss-20b 12.1 GB, gemma-4-26b-a4b 17.0 GB, qwen3.6-27b 17.6 GB, qwen3.6-35b-a3b 22.4 GB.</p>
      <p>The operating system also needs memory. On a 16 GB machine, keep the model under about 10 GB.</p>
    </Fragment>
  </Slide>

  <Slide section="Hardware" label="Memory bandwidth">
    <div class="c-1-10 stack-lg">
      <h2 class="t-display">Memory bandwidth</h2>
      <p class="t-head">
        Each new word reads the model from memory. Faster memory gives faster
        answers.
      </p>
    </div>
    <Fragment slot="notes">
      <p>Precisely: each new token reads the active weights once, so tokens per second is at most bandwidth divided by their size.</p>
      <p>MacBook Pro, M4 Pro, 273 GB/s: qwen3.6-27b reads 17.6 GB per token, upper limit about 15 tokens/s.</p>
    </Fragment>
  </Slide>

  <Slide section="Hardware" label="Mixture of experts">
    <div class="c-1-10 stack-lg">
      <h2 class="t-display">Mixture of experts</h2>
      <p class="t-head">
        These models read only part of their weights for each word. They need
        the memory of a large model and run at the speed of a small one.
      </p>
    </div>
    <Fragment slot="notes">
      <p>qwen3.6-35b-a3b has 35B parameters and reads about 3B per token. On the M4 Pro, the upper limit is about 130 tokens/s, against about 15 for the dense 27B.</p>
      <p>Measured on AMD Strix Halo, 128 GB: Qwen3 30B-A3B about 100 tokens/s, gpt-oss-120b about 55, dense Llama 3.3 70B about 5.</p>
      <p>Many current open models are mixtures of experts, including gpt-oss and the Qwen3 A3B models. This is why machines with large unified memory are useful for local inference.</p>
    </Fragment>
  </Slide>

  <Slide section="Hardware" label="Three kinds of machine">
    <div class="c-1-12 stack">
      <h2 class="t-display">Three kinds of machine</h2>
      <p class="t-head">Graphics card: fast memory, 24 to 32 GB</p>
      <p class="t-head">Unified memory: up to 512 GB, shared</p>
      <p class="t-head">Multiple cards: for labs and teams</p>
    </div>
    <Fragment slot="notes">
      <p>Graphics card: NVIDIA’s CUDA is supported by all inference and training tools. A used RTX 3090 (24 GB, $700 to $1,050) is a common start. An RTX 5090 (32 GB, $2,400 to $4,300) is the fastest consumer card.</p>
      <p>Unified memory: the processor and the graphics share one pool, with lower bandwidth than a card. Macs, AMD Strix Halo, NVIDIA DGX Spark.</p>
      <p>Multiple cards: tinybox, RTX Pro 6000 builds, DGX Station.</p>
      <p>Small models, such as the Runebender font models, also train on Apple Metal.</p>
    </Fragment>
  </Slide>

  <Slide section="Hardware" label="Example machines">
    <div class="c-1-12 stack">
      <h2 class="t-display">Example machines</h2>
      <p class="t-head">PC with an RTX 3090 or 5090</p>
      <p class="t-head">Strix Halo mini PC, Framework Desktop</p>
      <p class="t-head">NVIDIA DGX Spark</p>
      <p class="t-head">Mac Studio, M5 Max or M5 Ultra</p>
      <p class="t-head">tinybox, RTX Pro 6000, DGX Station</p>
    </div>
    <Fragment slot="notes">
      <p>US prices, October 2026. Memory shortages raised many of them during 2026.</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Used RTX 3090: 24 GB, 936 GB/s, $700 to $1,050 per card.</li>
        <li>RTX 5090: 32 GB, 1,792 GB/s, $2,400 to $4,300 per card.</li>
        <li>Strix Halo mini PC (AMD Ryzen AI Max+ 395, GMKtec, HP, Beelink, Minisforum): 128 GB, 256 GB/s, from about $2,000.</li>
        <li>Framework Desktop, Max+ 395: 128 GB, $3,449. PRO 495: 192 GB, $6,799 kit, ships November 2026.</li>
        <li>DGX Spark: 64 or 128 GB, 273 GB/s, $3,999 at launch, now higher. Two units link for 256 GB.</li>
        <li>Mac Studio M5 Max: 36 to 128 GB, 460 to 614 GB/s, from $2,499. M5 Ultra: 96 to 512 GB, 1.2 TB/s, from $5,499; 512 GB expected above $10,000.</li>
        <li>RTX Pro 6000 Blackwell: 96 GB per card, $9,000 to $16,000.</li>
        <li>tinybox green v2: 4 × RTX 5090, about $45,000.</li>
        <li>DGX Station GB300: 748 GB, 252 GB of it HBM at 7.1 TB/s, $85,000 to $160,000 from OEM partners.</li>
        <li>Mac Pro: discontinued March 2026, replaced by the Mac Studio.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Linux PC parts: 1000 W power supply for two cards, 64 GB system memory, 2 TB NVMe. llama.cpp splits a model across cards. My training PC has an RTX 2060 SUPER, 8 GB, about 100 s per epoch.</p>
    </Fragment>
  </Slide>

  <Slide section="Hardware" label="Choosing a machine">
    <div class="c-1-10 stack-lg">
      <h2 class="t-display">Choosing a machine</h2>
      <p class="t-head">
        Start with the computer you have. When a real task is too slow, buy
        for memory first.
      </p>
    </div>
    <Fragment slot="notes">
      <ul>
        <li>Train small font models: a Mac with Apple silicon, or an NVIDIA card with 8 GB or more.</li>
        <li>30B models at high speed: a 24 to 32 GB NVIDIA card, or a Mac with 48 GB or more.</li>
        <li>120B models on a desktop: 128 GB of unified memory.</li>
        <li>The largest open models: Mac Studio M5 Ultra, 256 to 512 GB.</li>
        <li>Teams, or training large models: RTX Pro 6000 builds, tinybox, DGX Station.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>My setup: MacBook Pro M4 Pro 48 GB for chat and coding with qwen3.6-35b-a3b. Linux PC with an RTX 2060 SUPER for training. Steam Deck runs qwen3.5-4b at 18 tokens/s.</p>
    </Fragment>
  </Slide>

  <Slide section="Hardware" label="Runtimes">
    <div class="c-1-10 stack-lg">
      <h2 class="t-display">Runtimes</h2>
      <p class="t-head">
        A runtime loads a model and runs it. Runebender uses llama.cpp for
        chat and font-ml for its own models.
      </p>
    </div>
    <Fragment slot="notes">
      <ul>
        <li>llama.cpp: runs GGUF files on Mac, NVIDIA, AMD, and CPU.</li>
        <li>MLX: Apple’s machine learning library.</li>
        <li>Ollama and LM Studio: desktop apps built on llama.cpp and MLX. The simplest first step outside Runebender.</li>
        <li>vLLM: serves many users at once on NVIDIA servers.</li>
        <li>PyTorch and Candle: training. font-ml uses Candle.</li>
      </ul>
    </Fragment>
  </Slide>

  <!-- ── Setup ── -->

  <Slide section="Setup" label="Installation" planned>
    <div class="c-1-10 stack-lg">
      <h2 class="t-display">Installation</h2>
      <p class="t-head">Mac: download the DMG from runebender.org.</p>
      <p class="t-head">Mac and Linux: install with Cargo.</p>
    </div>
    <Fragment slot="notes">
      <p>cargo install --git https://github.com/fontgarden/runebender</p>
      <p>cargo install --git https://github.com/eliheuer/font-ml --features metal (or cuda, or no feature for CPU)</p>
      <p>Linux (Debian or Ubuntu) needs system libraries first: libfontconfig-dev libwayland-dev libxkbcommon-dev libx11-dev libxcb1-dev libvulkan1 libssl-dev libglib2.0-dev libgtk-3-dev libxdo-dev libasound2-dev.</p>
      <p>Check with runebender --help and font-ml tasks. Compiling takes about fifteen minutes.</p>
    </Fragment>
  </Slide>

  <Slide section="Setup" label="Setup with a coding agent">
    <div class="c-1-10 stack-lg">
      <h2 class="t-display">Setup with a coding agent</h2>
      <p class="t-head">Give Claude Code or Codex this request:</p>
      <pre class="code">Read https://runebender.org/local-ai-setup-prompt.md
and set up Runebender for local AI on this machine.</pre>
    </div>
    <Fragment slot="notes">
      <p>The instructions tell the agent to inspect the machine first, ask before each download or install, leave your fonts unchanged, train a first shapes model, and report what it did.</p>
      <p>The chat model download is optional, about 800 MB. The agent does not open the editor unless asked.</p>
    </Fragment>
  </Slide>

  <Slide section="Setup" label="Exercises">
    <div class="c-1-10 stack-lg">
      <h2 class="t-display">Exercises</h2>
      <p class="t-head">Clone the demo repository and work through its five folders in order.</p>
      <pre class="code">git clone https://github.com/eliheuer/runebender-local-ai-demo</pre>
    </div>
    <Fragment slot="notes">
      <ul>
        <li>01-draw-from-text: train a shapes model from scratch, then fine-tune it.</li>
        <li>02-train-and-bolden: train a bolden model and bolden a new shape.</li>
        <li>03-chat-model: download an open-weight chat model and ask about the font.</li>
        <li>04-nodes: the same steps as a workflow in the node editor.</li>
        <li>05-real-font: the bolden loop on Virtua Grotesk.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Exercise 01 is the one to finish if there is time for only one. To reset the fonts, run git checkout -- fonts.</p>
    </Fragment>
  </Slide>

  <!-- ── Agents ── -->

  <Slide section="Agents" label="Agents over MCP">
    <div class="c-1-10 stack-lg">
      <h2 class="t-display">Agents over MCP</h2>
      <p class="t-head">
        Claude Code, Codex, and Cursor can work in the open editor. They
        propose edits, and you install them.
      </p>
      <pre class="code">runebender mcp --install</pre>
    </div>
    <Fragment slot="notes">
      <p>45 tools: read, measure, proof, propose point edits, and fork experiments. Five workflows as prompts: review-glyph, compatibility-check, spacing-pass, kerning-pass, draft-weight.</p>
      <p>The agent works on the open document, including unsaved changes. It reads first and proofs the result.</p>
      <p>Figma and Penpot have MCP servers, Glyphs has a third-party one, and FontLab 9 has an in-app chat. Runebender adds a review step and a safety contract: proposals, receipts, no saves.</p>
    </Fragment>
